# BMW 3 Series E30 two-door saloon, 1982-1994.
#
# The second 3-series: the first with the low waistline and slim pillars that
# define the 1980s BMW look, and the first 3-series sold with four doors and an
# estate. The two-door saloon is modelled, because that is the body in the
# side-profile reference.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BMW_3_Series_(E30)
#
# Dimensions 4.325 x 1.645 x 1.380 m, wheelbase 2.570 m, for the two-door
# saloon: the English Wikipedia dimension box cites the 1990 BMW facelift
# brochure, which gives a 2,570 mm wheelbase, 1,645 mm width, 4,320-4,460 mm
# length and 1,360-1,400 mm height across the body styles. Mirrors take the
# modelled width to 1.770 m.
#
# Colours. No BMW UK colour card for the E30 was located. The four names are
# the E30's own paint names from the E30 Zone paint-code register, an
# enthusiast compilation of BMW colour codes, not a UK price list:
#   Sterlingsilber metallic  244, listed 1987-1991
#   Diamantschwarz metallic  181, listed 1985-1991
#   Alpinweiss II            218, listed 1985-1991
#   Brillantrot              308, listed 1988-1991
# UK availability and exact years are therefore not established, and the
# header says so rather than implying brochure evidence. All four start at the
# 1985 model-year change, which is when the register first lists them.
# Weights silver 3, black 3, white 2, red 2, total 10, provisional pending the
# central rebase.
#
# Speed 200 km/h, between the 320i's 200 km/h and the 325i's 218 km/h; the M3's
# 235 km/h is not modelled. Comfort 110/495, on the line 330 + 1.5 x 110, a step
# below the E36's 115/503. Classes 1,2.
#
# Dates are road presence, not production: September 1982 intro, retires
# staggered 1999-2002 on a seventeen- to twenty-year tail.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/bmw-e30.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/bmw-e30.dae (53 objects). References:
# citycars/references/bmw-e30/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted low-poly deviations, recorded rather than corrected: the model is
# more angular than the photographs, and its cabin sits slightly further back
# on the body than the reference profile, so the bonnet reads a little long and
# the boot a little short. At 128 px it reads as an E30-shape saloon; the fault
# was judged not bad enough to spend a correction round on.

obj=citycar
